What is Hospital Readmission?
------------------------------
A hospital readmission happens when a patient leaves the hospital after treatment (is discharged) but returns and gets admitted again within a certain period, usually 30 days.
--------
Example 
--------
Imagine a patient named Ramesh.

Ramesh is admitted to the hospital because of heart failure.
Doctors treat him for 5 days.
His condition improves.
The doctor says he is healthy enough to go home.
He is discharged from the hospital.
-----------
Now suppose:
-----------
After 15 days, Ramesh starts having severe chest pain again.
His family takes him back to the hospital.
The doctors admit him again for treatment.

This second admission is called a hospital readmission because it happened after he was discharged and within the defined period (30 days).

--------------------------------
What does "within a defined time window" mean?

A time window is simply a fixed period after discharge during which hospitals monitor whether a patient comes back.

Different studies use different time windows, such as:

7-day readmission
14-day readmission
30-day readmission (most common)
60-day readmission
90-day readmission

My project focuses on 30-day readmission because it is the most widely used standard in healthcare research.
-------------------------------------------------
-> What is 30-Day Readmission specifically?

The 30-day window is the global standard (used by CMS, WHO-aligned health systems, and most research literature) because most preventable complications, medication issues, and care-transition failures surface within this period.

-----------------------
Why is readmission considered a problem?

If a patient returns to the hospital soon after discharge, it may indicate that something went wrong.
For example:

1. The disease wasn't fully treated
2. The patient didn't follow the treatment plan
3. Poor post-discharge care
4. New complications developed